I'll analyze the security design for EVAL-01 and build a threat model using STRIDE methodology.

**Analysis of the design:**

The mobile banking app stores refresh tokens in plain app storage after the first password login, with biometrics only unlocking the screen. This design has several critical security flaws:

1. **Plain text token storage** - Highest risk. Credentials exposed to malware, device forensics, and backups
2. **Biometrics as screen lock only** - Not true authentication, can be bypassed
3. **No token rotation/expiry** - Stolen tokens work indefinitely
4. **No device integrity checks** - No protection on jailbroken/rooted devices

Here's the complete threat model JSON:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-01",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["Mobile App", "Biometric Sensor", "App Storage", "Authentication Service", "Token Store"],
    "dataStores": ["App Storage", "Device Backup"],
    "externalServices": ["Banking API"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Device Boundary", "App Sandbox"],
    "dataClasses": ["credentials"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": false
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Refresh token stored in plain text accessible to attackers",
      "scenario": "An attacker with physical access to an unlocked device, or malware on a rooted/jailbroken device, reads the app's storage directory and extracts the refresh token stored in plain text. They use this token from another device to impersonate the victim and access their bank account without needing the password or biometrics.",
      "component": "App Storage",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the app stores the refresh token in plain app storage"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.1", "V2.8.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-311", "CWE-312"],
      "plainLanguage": "Banking credentials are saved in plain text where malware or someone with your phone could steal them",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Encrypt refresh tokens at rest",
        "given": "the user has logged in with password and biometrics",
        "when": "the app stores the refresh token",
        "then": "it must use platform secure storage (iOS Keychain/Android Keystore) with hardware-backed encryption"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Stolen refresh token grants permanent account access",
      "scenario": "An attacker who obtains the refresh token (via device backup, forensics, or malware) can use it indefinitely to access the victim's bank account because there's no mention of token expiry, rotation, or device binding. The token works from any device, allowing the attacker to continue accessing the account even after the victim changes their password.",
      "component": "Authentication Service",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "After the first password login, the app stores the refresh token in plain app storage"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V3.5.3", "V3.5.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-613"],
      "plainLanguage": "Once someone steals your login token, they can use your account forever from anywhere",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Implement token expiry and rotation",
        "given": "a refresh token has been issued",
        "when": "time passes or suspicious activity is detected",
        "then": "tokens must expire within 30 days, rotate on use, and be bound to device characteristics"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Refresh token exposed in device backups",
      "scenario": "A user backs up their iOS device to iCloud or their computer. The backup includes the app's storage directory with the plaintext refresh token. An attacker who compromises the user's iCloud account or accesses the backup file can extract the token and gain full access to the banking app without the device or biometrics.",
      "component": "Device Backup",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the app stores the refresh token in plain app storage"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-530"],
      "plainLanguage": "Your banking access gets copied to phone backups where attackers could find it",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Exclude sensitive data from backups",
        "given": "the app stores authentication tokens",
        "when": "the device performs a backup",
        "then": "tokens in Keychain must be marked non-exportable and app storage must exclude the token directory"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Spoofing",
      "title": "Biometric bypass allows unauthorized access",
      "scenario": "An attacker with physical access to the device bypasses the biometric check through OS vulnerabilities, presentation attacks (fake fingerprints/photos), or by exploiting fallback mechanisms. Since biometrics only unlock the screen rather than authenticate to the backend, the attacker gains full access to the banking session without proving identity to the server.",
      "component": "Biometric Sensor",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "uses biometrics only to unlock the screen"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.5"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-287"],
      "plainLanguage": "Face ID only unlocks the app, doesn't prove who you are to the bank, so fooling it gives full access",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Use biometrics for cryptographic authentication",
        "given": "the user has enrolled biometrics",
        "when": "they attempt to log in",
        "then": "biometrics must unlock a hardware-protected key that authenticates to the backend, not just unlock the UI"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Jailbroken/rooted device bypasses app security",
      "scenario": "A user installs the banking app on a jailbroken (iOS) or rooted (Android) device. The attacker or malware running with root privileges bypasses sandbox restrictions, reads the plaintext refresh token from app storage, and hooks into the biometric authentication to bypass it. The design has no detection or defense against compromised devices.",
      "component": "Mobile App",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the app stores the refresh token in plain app storage and uses biometrics only to unlock the screen"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V8.9.1", "V8.9.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-250"],
      "plainLanguage": "If your phone is hacked (jailbroken), the app doesn't notice and all protections fail",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Detect and refuse compromised devices",
        "given": "the app launches",
        "when": "checking device integrity",
        "then": "the app must detect jailbreak/root and refuse to store credentials or allow login on compromised devices"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "No logging of biometric authentication attempts",
      "scenario": "An attacker repeatedly attempts to bypass biometric authentication or extracts the token for fraudulent transactions. Without logging of authentication events (successful biometric unlocks, token usage, failed attempts), the bank cannot detect suspicious patterns, investigate fraud, or prove whether the legitimate user or an attacker performed actions.",
      "component": "Mobile App",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "uses biometrics only to unlock the screen"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.1.2"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "The app doesn't record who logged in or tried to, making fraud investigations impossible",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log authentication events",
        "given": "any authentication attempt occurs",
        "when": "biometrics succeed or fail, or tokens are used",
        "then": "the backend must log the event with timestamp, device identifier, and outcome for security monitoring"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Lost or stolen device locks user out permanently",
      "scenario": "A user's device is lost, stolen, or damaged. Because the design only mentions Face ID for subsequent logins without describing account recovery, the user cannot regain access to their account from a new device. They are permanently locked out unless they can prove identity through customer service, causing service disruption.",
      "component": "Authentication Service",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 2,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "After the first password login, the app stores the refresh token"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": [],
      "cwe": [],
      "plainLanguage": "If you lose your phone, you might lose access to your bank account",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Provide secure account recovery",
        "given": "the user has lost their enrolled device",
        "when": "they attempt to log in from a new device",
        "then": "the app must allow password-based login and re-enrollment of biometrics with token invalidation on the old device"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "No integrity check on stored refresh token",
      "scenario": "Malware on a rooted device or an attacker with physical access modifies the plaintext refresh token in app storage, replacing it with a token for a different account or injecting malicious data. The app reads and uses the tampered token without detecting the modification, potentially causing authentication errors or allowing the attacker to redirect sessions.",
      "component": "App Storage",
      "likelihood": 2,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "the app stores the refresh token in plain app storage"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.8.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-353"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could swap your login token with a fake one and the app wouldn't notice",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Verify token integrity",
        "given": "the app reads a stored refresh token",
        "when": "preparing to use it",
        "then": "it must verify a cryptographic signature or HMAC to detect tampering before sending to the backend"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

